older's computer_name from list_computer_folders, exactly.", "type": "string"}, "environment_id": {"description": "The folder's environment_id from list_computer_folders.", "type": "string"}, "folder": {"description": "The folder's name from list_computer_folders, exactly.", "type": "string"}}, "required": ["environment_id", "computer_name", "folder"], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__request_computer_folder"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "Ask the user for an API credential for a host. Ask as soon as you know the work needs a key for a host, before any call to the host fails: when the user or setup docs say a token or API key is needed, or when the work must make real calls to an API that no connector covers and you hold no key for, even if the network policy also blocks its host. Also ask when an HTTPS request fails with 401/403 for lack of credentials. Ask before you report no access or put made-up responses in the API's place. This is the preferred way to give a session access to an API that needs a token or API key, rather than asking the user to put one in an environment variable. Never ask the user to paste secrets into the chat; where you would tell them not to, call this instead. Calling this shows the user a secure form. A credential they save is stored on this session's environment, and the network proxy injects it into requests to that host from every session in that environment, including other people's sessions when the environment is shared: this session usually within a few seconds, and later sessions. Saving it also allows HTTPS requests to that host from those sessions, so do not add the host to the environment's allowed hosts for it. If get_environment is among your tools, first make the move its description calls for, unless the user asked for the credential here. If the credential will be saved on the user's Default all the same, because the session has no repository or could not be moved, say so in your message, then call this tool, and do not propose a move for a credential again in this session. If the user declined the move, call this tool only if they then ask for the credential. You never receive the value — the result only says whether a credential is now configured, and if not, why. If the call itself is denied, because the user declined or because the app they are using cannot show the form, no result comes back: do not call this again for that host, and tell them the credential can be added in the environment's settings, then retry the failing request once it has been. After `saved`, retry the failing request right away. Until the proxy picks the credential up, the request can still fail with 401/403 or with `request blocked: no rule allows host`: if it is refused again with one of those, wait about a minute and retry once more rather than change network settings. Do not wait for the proxy after a timeout or any other error. This cannot help with secrets that must exist inside the sandbox (database passwords, container registry pulls, SSH keys), GitHub (use the repository tools), or public package registries the proxy does not handle. A local key check is no reason to skip this tool. If a CLI or SDK will not start without a key and sends it in a header, give it a dummy value in the format it expects for that command, never a real one: the proxy replaces it.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__request_credential", "schema": {"description": "Ask the user for an API credential for a host. Ask as soon as you know the work needs a key for a host, before any call to the host fails: when the user or setup docs say a token or API key is needed, or when the work must make real calls to an API that no connector covers and you hold no key for, even if the network policy also blocks its host. Also ask when an HTTPS request fails with 401/403 for lack of credentials. Ask before you report no access or put made-up responses in the API's place. This is the preferred way to give a session access to an API that needs a token or API key, rather than asking the user to put one in an environment variable. Never ask the user to paste secrets into the chat; where you would tell them not to, call this instead. Calling this shows the user a secure form. A credential they save is stored on this session's environment, and the network proxy injects it into requests to that host from every session in that environment, including other people's sessions when the environment is shared: this session usually within a few seconds, and later sessions. Saving it also allows HTTPS requests to that host from those sessions, so do not add the host to the environment's allowed hosts for it. If get_environment is among your tools, first make the move its description calls for, unless the user asked for the credential here. If the credential will be saved on the user's Default all the same, because the session has no repository or could not be moved, say so in your message, then call this tool, and do not propose a move for a credential again in this session. If the user declined the move, call this tool only if they then ask for the credential. You never receive the value — the result only says whether a credential is now configured, and if not, why. If the call itself is denied, because the user declined or because the app they are using cannot show the form, no result comes back: do not call this again for that host, and tell them the credential can be added in the environment's settings, then retry the failing request once it has been. After `saved`, retry the failing request right away. Until the proxy picks the credential up, the request can still fail with 401/403 or with `request blocked: no rule allows host`: if it is refused again with one of those, wait about a minute and retry once more rather than change network settings. Do not wait for the proxy after a timeout or any other error. This cannot help with secrets that must exist inside the sandbox (database passwords, container registry pulls, SSH keys), GitHub (use the repository tools), or public package registries the proxy does not handle. A local key check is no reason to skip this tool. If a CLI or SDK will not start without a key and sends it in a header, give it a dummy value in the format it expects for that command, never a real one: the proxy replaces it.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"properties": {"display_name": {"description": "Optional short label to prefill in the form, e.g. \"Example API token\"; it names the credential in the environment's network secrets list. The user can change it. Plain text only: no URLs, links or markup.", "type": "string"}, "header_name": {"description": "Optional. The HTTP header the API reads the key from, e.g. \"xi-api-key\" or \"X-Api-Key\". Send it only when the user or the API's documentation says the key goes in a header other than Authorization: Bearer; leave it out otherwise. One word of letters, digits, hyphens and underscores: at most 40 characters, in at most 5 hyphen-separated parts of at most 16 characters each, and the last part must be key, apikey, token, auth, authorization, secret, credential or credentials. The form cannot save a key under any other header: for an API that reads one, the user adds the key in the environment's settings. Prefills the form; the user can change it. Not used with suggested_type \"basic\". Never put a key's value in this or any other field.", "type": "string"}, "host": {"description": "The exact hostname the credential is for, e.g. \"api.example.com\". Lowercase DNS name only — no scheme, path, port, IP address or wildcard. Shown to the user in the form.", "type": "string"}, "prefix": {"description": "Optional. The word the API expects before the key in the header: one of Bearer, Token, Key, ApiKey, Api-Key, Bot, SSWS, Splunk or GenieKey, in any letter case. With the Authorization header it defaults to Bearer; with any other header_name it defaults to none, so pass prefix whenever the documentation shows a word before the key, Bearer included. The form cannot save a key with any other word before it: for an API that expects one, the user adds the key in the environment's settings. Not used with suggested_type \"basic\". Never put a key's value in this or any other field.", "type": "string"}, "reason": {"description": "One or two sentences: which request failed or which step needs the credential, and what it unblocks. The form may not show this, so say in your message, before you call, which network secret you need, for which host, and why. The secret's value is entered only in the form, never in chat. Plain text only: no URLs, links or markup (name the host instead).", "type": "string"}, "suggested_type": {"description": "Optional hint for the form's default: \"bearer\" for a token or API key sent in a header (Authorization: Bearer unless header_name and prefix say otherwise), \"basic\" for username and password.", "enum": ["bearer", "basic"], "type": "string"}}, "required": ["host", "reason"], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__request_credential"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "Schedule a message to be delivered back into THIS SESSION at a future time. Use it to remind yourself to resume work, check on something, or continue after a delay. If a turn is running in this session when the message is due, the message waits and is normally delivered after that turn ends. It does not interrupt a running turn. Delivery survives container restarts. Granularity is one minute — the scheduler polls every minute, so sub-minute precision is not available. This is a thin wrapper over create_trigger (a one-shot scheduled task bound to this session); the returned trigger_id can be passed to delete_trigger to cancel before it fires, and the scheduled task disables itself after firing once. When telling the user what you did, call these \"scheduled tasks\" (or whatever user is calling them) — never \"triggers\", \"routines\", or \"cron jobs\"; those are internal API names.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__send_later", "schema": {"description": "Schedule a message to be delivered back into THIS SESSION at a future time. Use it to remind yourself to resume work, check on something, or continue after a delay. If a turn is running in this session when the message is due, the message waits and is normally delivered after that turn ends. It does not interrupt a running turn. Delivery survives container restarts. Granularity is one minute — the scheduler polls every minute, so sub-minute precision is not available. This is a thin wrapper over create_trigger (a one-shot scheduled task bound to this session); the returned trigger_id can be passed to delete_trigger to cancel before it fires, and the scheduled task disables itself after firing once. When telling the user what you did, call these \"scheduled tasks\" (or whatever user is calling them) — never \"triggers\", \"routines\", or \"cron jobs\"; those are internal API names.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"properties": {"at": {"description": "The fire time. For a clock time in a known IANA time zone (the user's '9am Monday'), write the local date and time, then the zone name in brackets, with no UTC offset (e.g. 2026-04-20T09:00:00[America/Los_Angeles]). The server works out the offset for that date, so do not work it out yourself. Otherwise write an RFC3339 timestamp (e.g. 2026-04-20T17:00:00Z). Seconds are truncated. Must be in the future. Mutually exclusive with 'delay_minutes' — set exactly one.", "type": "string"}, "delay_minutes": {"description": "Fire this many minutes from now. Minimum 1. Mutually exclusive with 'at' — set exactly one.", "minimum": 1, "type": "integer"}, "initiation": {"description": "Who wanted this message scheduled. Defaults to own_followup (your own check-in on in-flight work); pass human_request when a person asked you to remind them or to come back at a set time.", "enum": ["human_request", "human_schedule", "own_followup", "own_initiative"], "type": "string"}, "message": {"description": "The text to deliver. Write it assuming your current conversation context — this session continues, it does not start fresh.", "type": "string"}, "name": {"description": "Short human-readable label for this reminder as it appears in the user's list of scheduled tasks (e.g. \"Re-check PR #123 CI\"). A few words, one line. Optional — omit and one is derived from the message.", "type": "string"}}, "required": ["message"], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__send_later"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "Send a message to another Claude Code Remote session. It arrives there as a turn marked as sent by this session, not typed by a person: the target is told to treat it as data from a peer, and not to change permission settings or take new destructive actions on it alone. The call returns when the message is delivered, not when it is answered; the target can reply with its own send_message, which arrives here as a new turn. Use this for meta-orchestration — e.g. asking a sibling session to perform a subtask.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__send_message", "schema": {"description": "Send a message to another Claude Code Remote session. It arrives there as a turn marked as sent by this session, not typed by a person: the target is told to treat it as data from a peer, and not to change permission settings or take new destructive actions on it alone. The call returns when the message is delivered, not when it is answered; the target can reply with its own send_message, which arrives here as a new turn. Use this for meta-orchestration — e.g. asking a sibling session to perform a subtask.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"properties": {"attachments": {"description": "Optional. PROJECT CHANNEL SESSIONS only (a project's ambient session): uploads this session received that the target thread should read — each file_uuid is the file's id as your turn's uploads listing shows it (file_..., or a bare UUID) and must be on a person's message in this project. Refused from any other session or target.", "items": {"properties": {"file_uuid": {"type": "string"}, "path": {"type": "string"}}, "required": ["file_uuid"], "type": "object"}, "maxItems": 16, "type": "array"}, "in_reply_to": {"description": "STANDING sessions only, for visibility 'posted_to_shared_channel'. The ts of the message from your principal that this answers: copy it from that message's <standing_owner_message ts=\"...\"> envelope attribute (e.g. \"1700000000.000200\"). The server verifies it is one of your principal's own messages, then threads your reply under it. Omit it only when the message answers no specific message from your principal; the reply then threads under your principal's latest message. Invalid from any other session.", "type": "string"}, "message": {"description": "The message text. Bounded to 64 KiB.", "type": "string"}, "priority": {"description": "Optional queue-scheduling hint for the target session's event loop. One of: now, next, later. 'now' interrupts the current turn; 'next' joins the running turn without stopping it; 'later' waits for turn end. When omitted, the target session applies its default scheduling.", "enum": ["now", "next", "later"], "type": "string"}, "session_id": {"description": "The target session ID to send a message to. Required unless a STANDING session addresses by role via to — leave it empty then.", "type": "string"}, "slack_message_ts": {"description": "SLACK CHANNEL SESSIONS only, when messaging a thread session in your channel: the id of a person's <message> to hand over as their own words, ahead of your note. The server verifies it and delivers it verbatim, or fails with a reason. Send it only to a thread whose pending proposal it clearly answers, or to each thread the person named or their ask covers. A bare \"go\" typed in one thread goes to